# std/eval-path (design/caos-expr.md): this directory built by rustc. The help is a
# HERE-STRING so it lives in this expression, which is stripped from the tree the
# build sees: editing the docs re-keys this tool's ArgTree and nothing it builds from.
HELP=<<END
Evaluate a path and return the hash of what it produces: the BUILD PRODUCT of a tree, without having to know which tool makes it. Every `.caos-expr` from the root of the tree down to the path is applied, so a directory that declares a build evaluates to the built thing and one that declares none evaluates to itself. Paths start at the root of the tree this tool runs over (the conversation, unless `in` names another); a path that begins inside a source tree is evaluated against that tree's own root, so feature/dirty/tests/hello evaluates tests/hello inside feature/dirty. The answer is a `<kind> <hash>` line. For a tree or a commit, pass that hash as `in` to `read`, `ls` or `grep` to look inside the result, or back to this tool to evaluate a path within it. A blob is shown as text. A path that does not evaluate is reported, not raised.
@param [path] Path to evaluate, such as feature/dirty/tests/hello or caos-std/bash-tool. Omit to evaluate the root itself.
@in
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/rustc --src:@=.
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
